  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 AM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nShower and Storm Chances Return Tonight: With weak high pressure \nlingering over the region today expected dry conditions through the \ndaylight hours. This evening a short-wave impulse and surface cold \nfront will set off a cluster of thunderstorms across Minnesota where \nan increasingly unstable airmass will be in place. As these \nstorms track east towards central and north-central WI this \nevening the expectation is they will weaken due to diminishing \ninstability and weaker forcing. Areal extent of showers and \nstorms is also expected to become more isolated overnight with \neastward extend. May see a brief window for storms to re-intensify\nSaturday morning as some CAMs show a narrow corridor of \ninstability developing east of I-41. However, forcing will be \nlimited during this period so not confident storms will even \ndevelop. Weak high pressure is then progged to move in returning \ndry conditions for the remainder of the weekend.\n\nHeavy Rain and Storms Early Next Week: Global ensembles are in \ndecent agreement that a strong synoptic low-pressure system will \nbring a widespread soaking rain to the region late Sunday through \nMonday.  A strong mass response ahead of the surface low will \nincrease PWATs to 1.7-1.8\" which is above the 95th percentile of \nclimatology. With the abundant moisture and strong forcing NBM probs \nare showing a 30-60% chance for greater than 2\" of rain Monday, with \nthe highest chances across central WI. There is also a low end \nchance for stronger embedded thunderstorms, however, the strongest \ninstability is expected to remain south and west of the region which \nshould limit the severe potential. \n\nRest of the forecast: There continues to be low chances (20-30%) \nfor precipitation during the middle of next week as zonal flow\nsets up across the Great Lakes. This period could just as easily\nbe dry as it could be light showers as the models are uncertain\nhow close a surface boundary will be to the region. If the high\nwins out we stay dry, if the boundary is further north we could\nsee some showers at times. The one thing that seems certain is\nthat we will be noticeably cooler with highs only in the 60s.",
